= Jewish Prayer During the Days of Judgment\ Rosh Hashana 5708\ Harav Ben Ṣion Me’ir Ḥai Ouziel

#v(1em)
#align(right)[#emph[From “Beit Hakenesset”, the journal of\ the Great Synagogue, Tel
Aviv, Tishri 5708 \
//Text retrieved from #link("https://hebrewbooks.org/pdfpager.aspx?req=28392&st=&pgnum=15"), 16 & 17
  Translation: Simon Montagu]]
#v(1em)


Throughout the whole of our secular life we are plunged in the depths of
a dark and murky valley, in the battlefield of life with all its
vicissitudes and conflicts. A voice cries out in defeat: the voice of
the weak and destitute, physically and mentally oppressed by the
tyrannous and the wicked, saying “Surely there is no fear of God in this
place, and they will kill me”#footnote[Genesis 20:11];. Another voice
cries out in triumph: the voice of those with confidence in their
strength and power who reign in terror over those who are weaker than
they are – “the wicked encircle the righteous and thus justice is
perverted.”#footnote[Habakkuk 1:4]

There is no curse more dreadful and terrible than the lack of reverence
for God. It is the cause of carnage and destruction, of rage and
constant anguish, of the fear and mortal terror of man’s domination of
his fellow man which we saw in all its ghastliness in the last war, and
which continues to inflict its blows on the remnants of the Jewish
people who survived slaughter and persecution – many thousands of whom
are still experiencing suffering, oppression, famine and wasting away in
camps in the lands of persecution.

== The House of God
<the-house-of-god>
In this dark world the Jewish people in all the countries of their
dispersion found one sweet and precious corner full of the light of the
Holy One of Israel: the synagogue where they gathered every day, morning
and evening, and on every Shabbat and Holy Festival; where they found a
place filled with awe and with hope for the world; where they said in
the words of our Father Jacob, our holy ancestor: “How awesome is this
place! This is none other than the house of God, and this is the gate of
Heaven!”#footnote[Genesis 28:17]

When the Jewish people came together in this place, where the Holy
Presence of the God of Israel dwells, they rose above the darkness of
this world into a world where there was only light; their soul and
spirit were united with the God of Israel and they said, “Surely the
Lord is in this place! Surely God’s presence dwells in this place, and
God’s hidden light shines out from it on all creation in all its forms.”
Out of this sensation of pure and simple reverence for God their spirit
rallied, their soul regained its strength, and they spoke out in joy and
trembling: “You are awesome from your sanctuaries, O God, the God of
Israel; it is he that gives strength and power unto his people. Blessed
be God.”#footnote[Psalm 68:36]

In this respect the synagogue is the spring of living water from which
in every age and every period, in every situation, in all the scattered
dispersions of their exile, the Jewish people drew the strength and
power to endure and to accept all their troubles and tribulations with
love and grace and continue with full, fresh and youthful strength and a
supreme spiritual heroism unequalled in any nation or realm. They went
on affirming their living commitment to their unique mission: to
illuminate the world with the light of the true God and the light of
Israel’s Torah, a Torah of life and judgement for Israel and all the
nations.

== The House of Judgement
<the-house-of-judgement>
The synagogue is sacred on every day of the year, since it emanates
reverence and awe of holiness, as well as trust and hope in the
redemption of the Jewish people.

It is seven times more sacred and awesome on the days of judgement, the
ten days of repentance that begin on the first day of the seventh month.
Isaiah wrote “Seek the Lord while He is present, call upon him when He
is near”,#footnote[Isaiah 55:6] and our sages commented “These are the
ten days from Rosh Hashana to Yom Kippur”.#footnote[Rosh Hashana 18a]
They also said “Where is he present? In synagogues and houses of study.
Where is He near? In synagogues and houses of study. And what is more,
he stands over them, as it is written ‘God stands in the congregation of
the mighty, he adjudicates among the judges.’#footnote[Psalm
82:1];”#footnote[Yerushalmi Berachot 5:1] These two interpretations are
connected, because the synagogues and study halls, the place where God’s
presence dwells, are the place of nearness where we become closer to the
awe of God. That is the true closeness connecting the Jewish people with
their Father in Heaven, as the psalm says, “It is good for me to draw
near to God”,#footnote[Psalm 73:28] and as Ibn Ezra comments, “That is
true goodness”. That is what the sages meant when they wrote “Where is
He close? In the synagogues and houses of study.”

This presence and nearness of God is at its peak during the days of
judgement, when all created beings, and especially the Jewish people,
come before the judgement throne of the God who is sanctified by
righteousness; the days when the synagogues and houses of study that are
dedicated to Torah, to prayer, and to repentance are transformed into
the King of justice’s house of judgement. Thus he is not only present
and near, but standing over them: “God stands in the congregation of the
mighty, he adjudicates among the judges”.

There are two aspects of this sublime awe: awe of the God of Truth, who
permeates all worlds and is the moving force of all worlds, who watches
and sees the actions of every person and repays everyone according to
their deeds and the thoughts of their heart; and awe of the judgement
sanctified by righteousness, through which he is elevated and uplifted:
“The Lord of hosts is exalted by judgement and the holy God is
sanctified by righteousness.”#footnote[Isaiah 5:16] This awe raises us
to a supreme level of knowledge of the True God: “Know this day and take
it to heart, that the Lord is God.”#footnote[Deuteronomy 4:39] It leads
us to connect to God and to follow in his ways: “As he is merciful, do
you be merciful; as he is gracious, do you be gracious
…”#footnote[Shabbat 133b] And these two aspects are really one, as
Jeremiah says: “Let him that takes pride take pride in this, that he
understands me and knows me, that I am the Lord who exercises
lovingkindness, judgement and righteousness in the world, for these are
things in which I take pleasure, says the Lord.”#footnote[Jeremiah 9:24]
This awe becomes more intense in both its aspects during the days of
Judgement: it fills our soul with joy and bliss and unites us with our
Father in Heaven, the One Eternal God, as Nehemiah, Ezra the scribe, and
the Levites who interpreted the Torah said to all the people: “Do not
grieve, for the joy of the Lord is your strength”.#footnote[Nehemiah
8:10] This pure awe brings the heart and soul towards the love of God,
as our sages said: “My awe stems from my joy, my joy stems from my awe,
and my love is the greatest of all”#footnote[Eliyahu Rabba chap. 3];.
Through the light of this sublime awe, which flickers from the human
soul, the candle of the Lord,#footnote[Proverbs 20:27] and from the
holiness of God’s presence, which dwells within us and lights up our
gloomy darkness, we answer God’s voice when it calls us, saying “Return
unto me and I will return unto you.”#footnote[Malachi 3:7]

As this voice calls on us to awake, we run, young and old, in trembling
and loving longing, to the synagogue – to the house of God – to His
house of judgement – enfolded in awe, in a tallit of sanctity, lifting
up our hearts towards the holy sanctuary and the ark of the covenant,
towards the throne of judgement, repeating the words of prayer and of
praise which are the heritage passed down to us from our ancestors unto
all generations. In heartfelt prayer we pour out our sorrows before our
Father in Heaven, bewailing our long-drawn out suffering even as we pray
to him and plead for our redemption and liberation.

== The liturgy of the day
<the-liturgy-of-the-day>
The Rosh Hashana services are unique among all the Shabbat and Festival
services, in its content, in its form, and in its melodies that are so
appropriate to the holiness and reverence of the day, and in the
sounding of the Shofar with its rousing, terrifying, strengthening and
comforting call.

There is a common thread running through the whole service, a thread
made up of three strands:

1) The sanctity of God: “You are holy and your name is awesome, and
there is no God except for You, as it is written ‘The Lord of Hosts is
exalted in judgement, and the Holy God is sanctified in
righteousness’#footnote[Isaiah 5:16];”. “When the Holy One, Blessed be
He, executes judgement on his world he is is fearsome, elevated, and
praised.”#footnote[Zebaḥim 115b]

2) The establishment of the world under the rule of the Almighty: “Reign
over the whole world in your glory and be lifted up over the whole earth
in your splendour … and let everyone with breath in his lungs say ‘The
Lord the God of Israel is King, and his kingdom rules over all.’ ”

3) The redemption of Israel, which is the redemption of the Shechina: “O
Lord our God, may you rule swiftly over all your works on Mount Zion,
your glorious sanctuary, and in Jerusalem the city of your temple, as is
written in your holy writings ‘The Lord shall reign for ever, your God,
O Zion unto all generations. Praise the Lord!’#footnote[Psalm 146:16];”

This passage concludes with a sentence which is short, but contains
great depth and breadth: “And your word, our King, is true and
everlasting.”#footnote[Tur OḤ 582] The meaning of this sentence is not
obvious, because it does not say explicitly what is meant by “your
word”. Our rabbis disagreed about its meaning, and this disagreement led
to differences in the text. The “Mordechai”#footnote[Commentary on the
Talmud by Mordechai ben Hillel Hacohen \(Germany, ca. 1250–1298)] writes
that it refers to the verse “Your word stands firm in
heaven”:#footnote[Psalm 119:89] if You judged humanity with strict
justice, they would not remain in existence even for a moment, as the
verse says “On the day that you eat from it you will surely
die”#footnote[Genesis 2:17];, but you judge them with mercy, and you
have instructed them to judge their children with mercy. The “Kol
Bo”#footnote[Anonymous halachic compendium, Provence, 14#super[th]
century] interprets “your word” to mean “your promise”: you promised us
many times that you would redeem us and that you alone would reign, and
therefore we pray to you to fulfil your words, since your word is true
and everlasting.#footnote[Beit Yosef #emph[ad loc.];]

According to both of these interpretations, it would have been
sufficient to finish with the words “Your word is true and everlasting”,
and indeed R. Moses Isserles writes “we conclude ‘Your word is true and
everlasting’ ”#footnote[ReMA on Shulḥan ‘Aruch #emph[ad loc];., section
8];, which is also the text given by the RaMBaM#footnote[See Mishne
Torah, Seder Tefillot and Hilchot Shabbat 29:20];, but our teacher the
HIDA#footnote[Ḥaim Yosef David Azulai, 1724–1806] writes “our custom is
to say: ‘Your word, #emph[our King];, is true…’; this seems to have been
the ARI’s version”#footnote[Birkei Yosef #emph[ad loc.] section 11];,
and it is also the version in Sefer Ḥasidim \(chapter 258). In this
version, it means: “your word, when you said you would be our King, is
true and everlasting”, as in the verse “I am the Lord, your Holy One,
the Creator of Israel, your King”#footnote[Isaiah 43:15];, or “Thus says
the Lord the King of Israel and his redeemer, the Lord of
Hosts”#footnote[Isaiah 44:6];: the kingship of God which is unique to
the Jewish people has not deserted them and never will desert them, and
this is what will bring the knowledge of God and his kingship to the
whole world, as in Daniel’s vision of the end of days: “And the kingdom
and dominion, and the greatness of the kingdom under the whole heaven,
shall be given to the people who are the holy ones of the most High,
whose kingdom is an everlasting kingdom”#footnote[Daniel 7:27] or the
Jewish people’s proclamation, “For the Lord is our judge, the Lord is
our lawgiver, the Lord is our king; he will save us.”#footnote[Isaiah
33:22]

This is the same message that is implicit in the closing words of the
blessing said on the days of remembrance and judgement: “Your word, our
King, is true and everlasting”, like the words recited in the blessing
of redemption after the Shema‘: “It is true that You are the Lord our
God and the God of our fathers, our King and the King of our fathers,
our Rock and our Redeemer...”

And this is the essence of the liturgy on the days of judgement. This
sublime prayer uplifts the soul and the spirit to the Throne of Glory of
the Presence of the God of Israel, which is the Throne of Judgement of
the world’s only God and Judge.

In this context of spiritual elevation, the Shofar’s call is heard,
calling to mind two sublime and eternal memories:

1) The Binding of Isaac, which is literally reenacted for Isaac’s
descendants in every generation and in every age, providing a faithful
testimony of Israel’s glory and the presence of God’s kingship within
them, as in the verse “The Lord their God is with them and the trumpet
call of a king is among them.”#footnote[Numbers 23:21] This royal
fanfare emerges from the depths of the Jewish soul, which is imprinted
with His royal seal, radiating royal glory and pride upon their faces
and their actions, even as they are bound or their bodies are consigned
to the flames.

2) The memory of the Revelation of the Torah on Mount Sinai, when the
sound of the Shofar was heard with great power, terrifying the whole
world then and ever since, and causing all other sounds to fall silent
before it.

These associations of the days of remembrance and judgement are recalled
in words and in actions in the synagogue: in prayer, in admonition, and
in the sound of the Shofar. They are the Jewish people’s Defence Counsel
before the throne of judgement. They summon us all to repentance, to
stand again below Mount Sinai, where our ancestors proclaimed: “All that
the Lord has said, we will obey and we will listen.”#footnote[Exodus
24:7] Let us listen to the sound of the Shofar and make the same
proclamation: we will obey and we will listen. We will obey all God’s
commandments, and we will listen to the words of His Torah, since study
leads to action. And may it be in our favour that we obey all the
commandments that God gave us, so that we merit to hear the sound of the
Shofar of redemption, as Isaiah writes: “In that day it shall happen
that the great Shofar shall be sounded, and those who are in peril in
the land of Assyria, and those who are outcasts in the land of Egypt,
shall come and bow down before the #smallcaps[Lord] in the mountain of
the sanctuary: in Jerusalem.”#footnote[Isaiah 27:13]

I will conclude with a blessing: May all the House of Israel be written
and sealed for a good year, for a good life of peace and tranquillity,
for a year that will see the redemption of Israel in their land and in
their state, and the rebuilding of Jerusalem.

